Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Naturalistic late-1990s live-action. In a worn fifth-floor walk-up, NARRATOR, lean and dark-blond in a pizza-stained jacket, trails NICK upstairs. NICK is broad-shouldered, reddish-brown hair uncombed, scars on his chin and left brow, six grocery bags in each hand. His burnt-orange Trans Am waits below.

(SOUND: stairwell footsteps, grocery bags rustling, a distant television game show)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. His face looked like a puzzle with the pieces held together by sticky stuff from a glue gun.

MA, frail and brown-haired, sits close to the television with her pills in a straight row on the counter.

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. Old people start sleeping all day, and pretty soon they do not want to eat or go outside or nothing.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

NARRATOR parks his bicycle as SIONNA trips on the broken cement and brushes his shoulder. She is pale, slender, and late twenties, with shoulder-length brown hair, watery light-blue eyes, a fish necklace, a black V-neck shirt, jeans, blue tennis shoes, and a backpack.

(SOUND: bicycle chain ticking, traffic hissing over wet pavement)

NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

NARRATOR
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

NARRATOR
Want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks, I do not eat pizza. But if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

Sionna writes her number on his receipt, then walks away and looks back over her shoulder.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College students bend over books and coffee. Sionna, now in a loose green hooded top, sits across from NARRATOR at a small back table.

(SOUND: espresso grinder whirring, cups clinking, low student chatter)

NARRATOR
I am reading Wuthering Heights. It is about this guy and girl who grew apart but were really in love with each other.

SIONNA
The environment is like its people. Wild and unpredictable. The love Heathcliff has for Catherine never changes, but that kind of love destroys him.

NARRATOR
I dreamed I was on a horse with you. Old clothes, a forest, a field. It felt like it was my life for real.

SIONNA
Maybe you were dreaming about something from a past life.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something inside of me was coming awake. I could talk to Sionna about anything, and she would not think it was stupid.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

NARRATOR strains on a purple mat while Sionna moves easily beside him. A community-college application sticks out of his backpack.

(SOUND: measured breathing, floorboards creaking beneath shifting mats)

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what is going on on your mat.

NARRATOR
My practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA
That is why they call it practice.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I began reading, meditating, and going to school. For once, I felt like a person by myself, without Nick, without Ma, without my job and belongings.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Three days after a hospital scare, MA sits beneath her afghan beside a blue day-by-day pill box. NICK, red-faced in his brown suede jacket and work boots, stares at a missed doctor’s appointment slip.

(SOUND: pillbox compartments snapping shut, the television audience applauding)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment, you ass. Where the hell were you?

NARRATOR
I was with someone. I met a girl.

NICK
A girl. That is worth all this? Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

NARRATOR
Somebody has to make sure she stays on track.

NICK
Man, you shoulda been a nurse.

NARRATOR
Stop it, Nick.

NICK
Do not you ever talk that way about MY mother, you little fuck.

Nick slaps NARRATOR across the head.

(SOUND: the slap cracks, MA gasps, the apartment door slams)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In that split second, I knew me and Ma and Sionna would be just fine without him.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Hard rock shakes a crowded pool room. NICK spots his ex-girlfriend with another man and stalks toward them. NARRATOR blocks his path.

(SOUND: AC/DC blasting from corner speakers, pool balls breaking)

NARRATOR
Leave it, man. You do not even know this guy.

NICK
Kiss my ass. Did I ask your opinion?

NARRATOR
Stop it. You are crazy as shit. No one cares about your ego, Nick.

Nick’s fist catches NARRATOR across the face. Bar patrons freeze as JOE'S BOUNCER rushes in.

(SOUND: cue sticks clatter, chairs scrape back, NARRATOR'S breath pounds as he runs)

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

NARRATOR, shaken and bleeding at the lip, lies with his head in Sionna’s lap on her green suede sofa. Her pale blue eyes hold steady on him.

(SOUND: rain tapping the windows, a refrigerator humming in the next room)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one does that who really cares about you.

NARRATOR
I love Nick. He always took care of us.

SIONNA
He is a controlling ass, and you are showing him that you do not need him. You are becoming a bravely compassionate being.

NARRATOR
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you, and to honor it. To be the best person you can while you are here.

INT. COMMUNITY COLLEGE CLASSROOM - DAY

Months later, NARRATOR takes notes among students young and old. His dark-blond hair is neater now; books, a used laptop, and a bike helmet crowd his desk.

(SOUND: chalk scratching, notebook pages turning, a campus bell ringing outside)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I woke up with a purpose: taking care of Ma, learning, and loving Sionna. I was finding out how much there was out there that I did not know.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

MA eats peach pie while SIONNA clears plates. NICK enters, larger than the room, his scarred face tightening when NARRATOR mentions school.

(SOUND: forks scraping plates, the Trans Am engine ticking outside)

NARRATOR
I got a loan. I am going to community college for business. I do not want to work in a pizza place my whole life.

NICK
How the hell will you pay for that? You are letting this chick tell you what to do.

SIONNA offers NICK her hand.

SIONNA
I am Sionna. Nice to meet you.

NICK
I hear you are a university lady.

SIONNA
There are more females in college today than males. Diversity helps us learn. We are all humans trying to live on this planet and make a difference.

NICK
Women cannot stay home and take care of their kids anymore.

SIONNA
Many women have no choice. You cannot keep a good woman down.

NICK stares at her, beaten by words he cannot answer.

Weeks pass in the same room: NICK brings MA meals, lays out her pills, and escorts her toward the waiting Trans Am for evening Mass.

(SOUND: the Trans Am V8 starts, MA'S church shoes shuffle down the hall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You do not just go from tough guy to petting your mother’s head and taking her to church. What had gotten into Nick?

EXT. WERNER AND LOWE INTERSECTION - NIGHT

NICK drives the flame-painted Trans Am through the intersection. MA rides beside him with a church bulletin folded in her lap.

(SOUND: engine roar, tires skidding hard on asphalt)

Another car hits the Trans Am broadside. Metal buckles. Glass erupts across the street.

(SOUND: impact thunder, steam hissing, distant sirens closing in)

Rescuers cut into the crushed car. MA lies still in the passenger seat.

INT. MILWAUKEE COUNTY SHERIFF'S STATION - DAY

NARRATOR, pale and unshaven, listens to five blinking messages at a crowded front desk. OFFICER DUPUIS, a large man with graying hair and a thin mustache, guides him to a chair.

(SOUND: message-machine beeps, telephones ringing, fluorescent lights buzzing)

OFFICER DUPUIS
There has been a serious accident involving your brother and your mother. Your mother passed away at the scene. Your brother is in serious condition at Frodo Hospital.

NARRATOR
Can I have a ride to the hospital?

OFFICER DUPUIS
Toxicology is pending. We believe your brother may have been drinking.

NARRATOR crushes a Styrofoam cup of water in his trembling hand.

(SOUND: cup crackling, a chair leg scraping sharply across tile)

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

A light maple casket rests above an open grave. NARRATOR, in a black coat, drops a handful of dirt onto its lid.

(SOUND: dirt landing with a muted thunk, cold wind moving through bare trees)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
One day you are talking to her. The next day you are putting her in a box.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL - DAY

Five days later, NICK lies yellow and bruised beneath white sheets, oxygen in his nose, chest tube draining into a plastic box. His right leg is raised and immobilized.

(SOUND: heart monitor beeping, oxygen hissing through nasal tubing)

NICK
I guess I really fucked up, man. I did not mean to have three Jack and Cokes before I remembered I needed to take her to church.

NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
You think I meant to kill Ma? You son of a bitch.

NARRATOR
Think about it, Nick.

NARRATOR turns and leaves his brother trapped in the bed.

(SOUND: the hospital door closes, monitor beeps continue behind it)

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Months later, SIONNA cooks alone in her loose green top. NICK, still huge despite his limp, stands in the doorway with his weathered face and wild reddish-brown hair.

(SOUND: deadbolt sliding, vegetables sizzling in a pan)

NICK
If it was not for you messing around with my brother, Ma would be alive. You got two things to do: get away from him and come up with some money.

SIONNA
This has nothing to do with me. Let us talk about this rationally.

NICK crowds her against the hallway wall and clamps one hand around her throat. With the other, he flicks open a switchblade.

(SOUND: knife blade clicking open, SIONNA'S breath squeaking against his grip)

NICK
If I wanted to kill you, I could kill you right now. Break it off with him tonight.

The front door bursts open. NARRATOR, lean and dark-blond in his delivery jacket, grabs for NICK'S knife arm.

NARRATOR
Nick!

The struggle jerks the blade into SIONNA'S upper chest near her left shoulder. Blood spreads across her shirt as NICK tears free and flees.

(SOUND: SIONNA falls hard, NARRATOR dials frantically, sirens begin in the distance)

NARRATOR presses both hands over the wound.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My brother stabbed her. He stabbed her.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Months later, SIONNA sits in a shoulder brace, her hair limp around a face emptied of its former light. Court papers naming NICK'S sentence lie unopened on the table.

(SOUND: wall clock ticking, a suitcase zipper drawing closed)

NARRATOR
You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
I cannot feel me. I cannot feel myself anymore.

NARRATOR
Stay here. We can work things out.

SIONNA
As long as there is any doubt, I cannot go on with you.

SIONNA carries her suitcase through the front door. NARRATOR remains on the sofa as her car pulls away.

(SOUND: tires leaving wet gravel, the clock continuing alone)

INT. TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA, SEATTLE - DAY

Eight years later, NARRATOR is a stronger man in his mid-thirties, dark-blond hair cut neat, supervising a busy Northwest pizza shop. Delivery drivers check GPS screens while pizzas slide from glowing ovens.

(SOUND: order tickets printing, oven timers chiming, delivery bags zipping)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Thanks to Tony, I did pretty well for myself. I owned a chain now: Tony's Boy's Pizza. But eight years of loneliness and hatred for my brother still lived under everything.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles ring NARRATOR'S mat. He closes his eyes as the room dissolves into a vision of green hills and a dark-skinned SAGE in flowing white cloth.

(SOUND: a singing bowl vibrating, synchronized breaths filling the room)

SAGE (V.O.)
Stop judging things. Things are neither good nor bad. This is an illusion.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Holding onto resentment kept me in my own prison. I finally had the key to unlock it.

NARRATOR opens his eyes, reaches for his phone, and books a flight to Milwaukee.

(SOUND: phone keys tapping, rain whispering against the studio windows)

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - DAY

Behind thick Plexiglas, NICK sits in an orange prison uniform. He is scrawny now, gray at the temples, his old scars buried beneath wrinkles. Across from him, NARRATOR grips a yellow telephone receiver.

(SOUND: steel doors clanging, amplified voices echoing through the visitation room)

NICK
Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR
I am here to tell you that I am no longer interested in seeing you or hearing anything you have to say. You killed my mother. You held me down my whole life. You tried to kill the love of my life.

NICK lowers his eyes.

NARRATOR
You are an asshole, Nick. But I forgive you. I forgive you because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

NICK'S eyes fill. Tears run down his cheeks.

NICK
I am so sorry. You are right. I am a huge asshole.

NARRATOR
I still love you for being my brother. I want you to get better.

NICK
What happened with you?

NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. I did it, Nick. I really did it.

NICK
I am proud of you, man.

A guard signals that time is over. NARRATOR hangs up as NICK is led away.

(SOUND: receiver clacking into place, keys jangling, steel doors locking)

INT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

NARRATOR joins the boarding line. His ticket reads: PETER OLSON.

(SOUND: boarding-pass scanner chirping, jet engines droning beyond the gate)

NICK (V.O.)
President Olson.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That is me. President Peter Olson. This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
